sub("Philo's seven stand on one lampstand; the Apocalypse's seven lampstands stand apart, each answerable for itself. Dependence is left undecided (0.5).",
    "Philo's seven stand on one lampstand; the Apocalypse's seven lampstands stand apart, each answerable for itself. The conjunction is specific, clustered, transformed and ordered, and the paper states the dependence it carries: the opening vision of the Apocalypse and its seven letters are built on the Philonic reading of Zechariah's lampstand and high priest (16.7). The route, Philo's text or a tradition that carried his reading, is left open. At the centre of that reading is a cut. Twice the Septuagint speaks the name Ἀνατολή, and both times it speaks it to Ἰησοῦς the high priest (Zech 3:8; 6:11–12). Philo takes the name for the Logos and withholds the name of the one it was spoken to (4.4). The Apocalypse sets a figure robed as Zechariah's Ἰησοῦς in the midst of the lampstands and names him (7a.9).")

# ---- 0. method and limits
sub("Where it joins two texts, the paragraph says so: \"the chain, each link Philo's, the joining the reading's\" (7a.6); \"set side by side, without a claim of dependence\" (7b.7).",
    "Where it joins two texts, the paragraph says so: \"the chain, each link Philo's, the joining the reading's\" (7a.6). Where it argues dependence, it states the criteria and the evidence in one place (16.7).")
replace_para("0.5. ", "0.5. The paper does not decide the date of the Apocalypse or of any letter, the identity of Zechariah's Ἀνατολή, or the historicity of any person, and it reads no motive. It argues one dependence on the evidence it assembles: the Apocalypse's opening vision and its seven letters are built on the Philonic construction of Zechariah's lampstand, high priest and Logos (16.7). Whether that construction reached the Apocalypse from Philo's text or through a tradition that carried it is left open, and so is every other dependence (Justin on Philo; the letters to churches on the Apocalypse, 15.3). A text late in its transmission (the Ginza in its compilation; the Haran Gawaita, copied in the Arab era) is dated where it is used and read for what it offers itself as (13.0).")

# ---- 2. counts
after_para("2.5. ", "2.6. Ζαχαρίας in the 31 seated works: zero. Philo names neither the prophet whose oracle he quotes at Conf. 62 nor the high priest Ἰησοῦς, son of Iosedek, to whom the oracle is spoken. The ten forms of 2.1 all name the other Ἰησοῦς, the son of Nun.")

# ---- 4. Zechariah
sub("(In the seated text layer, apparatus is run into the verse lines at 3:8 and 6:11; the clause above is asserted, the rest of the line is flagged for repair.)",
    "(In the seated text layer, apparatus is run into the verse lines at 3:8 and 6:11; the clause above is asserted, the rest of the line is flagged for repair. The end of 3:8 is read in the TEI of the same seat, page 78.)")
after_para("4.1. ", "4.1a. The same Ἰησοῦς, two chapters earlier. Zechariah 3 is a vision of him. He stands in filthy garments before the angel (3:3); the command is given, \"ἐνδύσατε αὐτὸν ποδήρη\" (3:4), and a clean κίδαρις is set on his head (3:5); he is promised \"ἀναστρεφομένους ἐν μέσῳ τῶν ἑστηκότων τούτων\" (3:7); he is addressed, \"ἄκουε δή, Ἰησοῦς ὁ ἱερεὺς ὁ μέγας\", and told \"ἰδοὺ ἐγὼ ἄγω τὸν δοῦλόν μου Ἀνατολήν\" (3:8); a stone is set before him, \"ὁ λίθος ὃν ἔδωκα πρὸ προσώπου Ἰησοῦ, ἐπὶ τὸν λίθον ἕνα ἑπτὰ ὀφθαλμοί εἰσιν\" (3:9). The next vision is the lampstand of seven lamps (4:2). Twice the Septuagint speaks the name Ἀνατολή, at 3:8 and 6:12, and both times the one spoken to is Ἰησοῦς the high priest. The lampstand stands between the two oracles.")
replace_para("4.3. ", "4.3. The oracle is spoken to Ἰησοῦς, crowned. Whether \"a man, Rising his name\" is the crowned Ἰησοῦς or a second figure is the open question of 1.3 and 1.5; the Greek text fixes the address: πρὸς αὐτόν, to the one just crowned. Which Ἰησοῦς he is, is not open. He is the son of Iosedek, the high priest of the return, a different man from Moses' successor, the son of Nun. Every Ἰησοῦς in Philo is the son of Nun (2.1); the Ἰησοῦς of Zechariah is the one Philo never names (2.6).")
replace_para("4.4. ", "4.4. Philo quotes the oracle and withholds the name at the place it attaches. The passage is about a Ἰησοῦς: both oracles of Ἀνατολή are spoken to him (4.1a). Philo takes from the passage the name he gives the Logos and cuts the quotation one verse below its addressee. He names neither Ἰησοῦς nor Zechariah; the prophet becomes \"one of the companions of Moses\" (τῶν Μωυσέως ἑταίρων τινός). The cut falls on one name, and it is the name the Logos-name was spoken to. Philo's doctrine allows names of approach for the Logos, Israel among them (5.4); the one name this oracle carries is the one left out. That Philo had 6:11 in view is a statement about his mind and is not made. What the citation does is a fact of the text: it removes the addressee. The paper reads the removal as the most significant act in it.")
after_para("4.4. ", "4.4a. The socket (the author, 2026-11-11: \"the socket in philo is the reader supplying the name of the missing referent, jesus\"). A reader who knows Zechariah supplies what the citation removes. The oracle is quoted; the one it is spoken to is withheld; whoever receives the verse whole fills the place. The Apocalypse states the same structure as a gift: a name written on a stone, \"ὃ οὐδεὶς οἶδεν εἰ μὴ ὁ λαμβάνων\" (2:17; 7b.10).")
replace_para("4.5. ", "4.5. The companions of Moses. The commentary of 9 November 2025 turned on this phrase (\"He did not leave us names. He left only the gesture.\"). At Conf. 62 the companion is Zechariah. The phrase that stands in the place of the two withheld names names a class, and the first of that class is the other Ἰησοῦς: in the Septuagint Moses' attendant, \"ὁ δὲ θεράπων Ἰησοῦς υἱὸς Ναυὴ\" (Exod 33:11), in Philo \"ὁ φοιτητὴς αὐτοῦ καὶ μιμητὴς τῶν ἀξιεράστων ἠθῶν Ἰησοῦς\" (Virt. 66). The substitute points to the son of Nun as the name it removes points to the son of Iosedek. The two Ἰησοῦς meet at the cut. The joining is the reading's (7a.8).")

# ---- 7a. the two roads, and the figure in the midst
after_para("7a.7. ", "7a.8. The two roads. Philo reaches the Logos from a Ἰησοῦς twice, by different roads, and keeps the roads apart. The first is the high priest's. The oracle spoken to Ἰησοῦς the high priest gives the Logos its name Ἀνατολή (Conf. 62–63); the Logos is itself the high priest, \"λέγομεν γὰρ τὸν ἀρχιερέα οὐκ ἄνθρωπον ἀλλὰ λόγον θεῖον εἶναι\" (Fug. 108); Zechariah's Ἰησοῦς is robed in the ποδήρης (3:4), and Philo's ποδήρης is the robe of the high priest who is the Logos, the air, the region of the messengers (Vit. Mos. II.117–121; Fug. 110; 16.5). Wisdom has the cosmos on the same robe and names on stone in the same verse: \"ἐπὶ γὰρ ποδήρους ἐνδύματος ἦν ὅλος ὁ κόσμος. καὶ πατέρων δόξαι ἐπὶ τετραστίχου λίθου γλυφῆς\" (Wis 18:24; Swete's seat numbers the verse 19:24). On this road the name Ἰησοῦς is withheld (4.4). The second road is the son of Nun's: the renaming of Hosea struck as a coin, \"τὸν ποιὸν εἰς ἕξιν μεταχαράττων\" (Mut. 121), and the imitator who takes Moses' type (Virt. 66). On this road the name is given, and it is a man's. Hebrews joins the roads in seven verses: \"εἰ γὰρ αὐτοὺς Ἰησοῦς κατέπαυσεν\" (4:8), the son of Nun; the dividing Logos (4:12; 16.4a); \"ἀρχιερέα μέγαν διεληλυθότα τοὺς οὐρανούς, Ἰησοῦν τὸν Υἱὸν τοῦ Θεοῦ\" (4:14), with the title the Septuagint gives Zechariah's Ἰησοῦς, \"ὁ ἱερεὺς ὁ μέγας\" (Zech 3:8). Justin joins them by name, and calls the vision of Zechariah 3 an apocalypse: \"ὅνπερ τρόπον διὰ τοῦ Ἰησοῦ ὀνόματος τῷ Ναυῆ υἱῷ καὶ δυνάμεις καὶ πράξεις τινὰς προκηρυσσούσας τὰ ὑπὸ τοῦ ἡμετέρου κυρίου μέλλοντα γίνεσθαι πεποιηκέναι ἔφη, οὕτω καὶ τὴν ἐπὶ τοῦ ἐν Βαβυλῶνι Ἰησοῦ ἱερέως γενομένου ἐν τῷ λαῷ ὑμῶν ἀποκάλυψιν\" (Dial. 115.4), and then \"ἵνα τὸν λόγον τὸν περὶ τῆς ἀποκαλύψεως Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ τοῦ ἁγίου ἀποδιδῶ ὑμῖν\" (116.1). Justin's Zechariah proclaims \"ἀποκεκρυμμένως\" (115.1). The Apocalypse opens \"Ἀποκάλυψις Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ\" (1:1).")
after_para("7a.8. ", "7a.9. The figure in the midst. The conventional sources of Rev 1:13 come first: the man among six in Ezekiel's vision, \"ἐν μέσῳ αὐτῶν ἐνδιδυκὼς ποδήρη\" with a girdle at his loins (Ezek 9:2), and Daniel's son of man. Beside Ezekiel's, the one other wearer of the ποδήρης in a Septuagint vision is Ἰησοῦς the high priest (Zech 3:4), in the chapter of the stone with seven eyes (3:9), before the lampstand of seven lamps (4:2). Revelation's figure stands \"ἐν μέσῳ τῶν λυχνιῶν\", \"ἐνδεδυμένον ποδήρη\" (1:13), and the book names him: \"Ἀποκάλυψις Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ\" (1:1); \"Ἐγὼ Ἰησοῦς\" (22:16). Ezekiel's six with one in their midst is Philo's six divided by the seventh in the middle (Her. 216–219). The Apocalypse sets the robe of Zechariah's Ἰησοῦς on the one in the midst of Zechariah's lampstand, made seven, and gives the name that Philo's citation withholds. Each piece is in the seated texts; the joining is the reading's, and its dependence is argued in 16.7.")

# ---- 7b. the white stone
replace_para("7b.1. ", "7b.1. Absences first, counted in the 31 seated works: no λευκὴ ψῆφος and no white stone; no καινὸν ὄνομα, no \"new name.\" ψῆφος occurs six times, always the juror's vote; where the vote is God's it is the vote of condemnation: \"τὴν καταδικάζουσαν ψῆφον οἴσει\" (Deus 75), \"τὴν καταδικάζουσαν ἀπὸ τούτων ψῆφον ἤνεγκε\" (Migr. 115). The Quaestiones, surviving whole only in Armenian, are not seated and are not searched here.")
sub("7b.7. Set side by side, without a claim of dependence.", "7b.7. Set side by side; the claim of dependence is made in 16.7.")
sub("The white stone is the one item the Apocalypse has that Philo does not; every other term of Rev 2:17 and 19:12–13 has a Philonic counterpart in the high-priestly and manna exegesis.",
    "Every term of Rev 2:17 and 19:12–13 has a Philonic counterpart in the high-priestly and manna exegesis; the white stone, the one term without a Philonic phrase, is joined in 7b.9.")
after_para("7b.8. ", "7b.9. The white stone, joined. The ψῆφος of Rev 2:17 is a juror's pebble, and its colour is the verdict: \"Mos erat antiquus niveis atrisque lapillis, / his damnare reos, illis absolvere culpa\" (Ovid, Met. 15.41–42), black to condemn, white to acquit. In the New Testament the word occurs twice: the persecutor's condemning vote, \"ἀναιρουμένων τε αὐτῶν κατήνεγκα ψῆφον\" (Acts 26:10), and the white stone given to the conqueror (Rev 2:17). Philo has the divine vote only as condemnation (7b.1), and gives the condition under which God casts it: \"εἰ γὰρ βουληθείη ὁ θεὸς δικάσαι τῷ θνητῷ γένει χωρὶς ἐλέου\" (Deus 75), if he would judge without mercy. The Apocalypse gives the other vote. On the white pebble a name is written: Philo's stones carry names struck as on coins (7b.4), and the high priest's stones in Wisdom carry \"πατέρων δόξαι\" on an engraved stone (Wis 18:24; 7a.8). The name is new: Philo's new name is a re-striking, and the Logos gives it (Mut. 71, 87, 121). The manna beside it in the same verse is Philo's Logos, eaten \"ἕνεκα τοῦ τυπῶσαι βεβαιότερον τὴν διάνοιαν\" (Sacr. 86). The three gifts of 2:17 are three stampings in Philo's vocabulary, and the stone is the divine vote turned from condemnation to acquittal, given at the station of the sword (11.4). Philo has the stone, the name, the newness and the vote. The Apocalypse adds the colour, and turns the verdict.")
after_para("7b.9. ", "7b.10. The name its receiver knows (the author, 2026-11-11: \"thats the socket. a name only the one who receives it will know. the socket in philo is the reader supplying the name of the missing referent, jesus\"). Rev 2:17: \"ὃ οὐδεὶς οἶδεν εἰ μὴ ὁ λαμβάνων\". Philo quotes the Ἀνατολή oracle and withholds the name of the one it is spoken to (4.4); the name is held by whoever receives the verse whole (4.4a). The Apocalypse makes the structure a gift: a written name its receiver knows. At 19:12–13 the rider's written name is known to himself, \"ὃ οὐδεὶς οἶδεν εἰ μὴ αὐτός\", and his name is called the Logos of God; the name the book gives its speaker, at its first verse and its last chapter, is Ἰησοῦς (1:1; 22:16). The name Philo's citation leaves for its receiver to supply is the name the Apocalypse writes.")

# ---- 11. the sequence
sub("The order of the letters is the circuit of the cities from Ephesus; it follows neither the Chaldean order of the planets nor the order of the weekdays, and the mapping does not claim an order. What the seven give is a distributed configuration: seven differentiated letters fitted to seven planetary identities.",
    "The order of the letters is the circuit of the cities from Ephesus. The governing mapping (#1101) claims an order of its own: the seven are \"the seven spheres the Logos must pass through on its descent into matter.\" Read against the Chaldean order, the order of Philo's lampstand with the sun in the middle (Her. 222–224), the circuit has a shape (11.5b).")
after_para("11.5a. ", "11.5b. The sequence. The Chaldean order, from the outermost: Saturn, Jupiter, Mars, Sun, Venus, Mercury, Moon. The letters, on the governing mapping: Ephesus Mercury, Smyrna Moon; Pergamum Mars, Thyatira Venus; Sardis Saturn, Philadelphia Jupiter; Laodicea Sun. The six fall in three pairs, each pair adjacent in the order once the Sun is withdrawn: the two lowest (Mercury, Moon), the Sun's two neighbours (Mars, Venus), the two highest (Saturn, Jupiter). Each pair runs downward, the higher sphere first. The pairs climb, lowest, middle, highest. The seventh letter goes to the middle, the Sun, the station whose speaker is \"ἡ ἀρχή\" (3:14), the first of the Logos's names (Conf. 146). It is Philo's lampstand read in pairs, the six about the middle, closing on the seventh that divides (Her. 216–219). Of the 5,040 orders of seven, 6 have the paired shape with the Sun last (the three downward pairs in any order); 1 has the pairs climbing as here. The figure describes; it does not test. The mapping was fitted to the letters' content (11.4), and the road between the cities fixes the order. What it gives is what Charles counted as design at 21:19–20, a sequence with a direction (16.6). The constraint test of 11.5a carries a second measure from here: whether an assignment fixed from outside the archive recovers the shape.")
sub("The test, run properly: fix the seven profiles from a source outside the archive (a planetary signification list of the period, seated), score each letter against each profile, record every tie.",
    "The test, run properly: fix the seven profiles from a source outside the archive (a planetary signification list of the period, seated; the Haran Gawaita's planetary communities, 13.6), score each letter against each profile, record every tie.")

# ---- 13. the Haran Gawaita as key
sub("## 13. The Haran Gawaita\n\n13.1.", "## 13. The Haran Gawaita\n\n13.0. What the text offers itself as (the author, 2026-11-11: \"its transmission is late, but it *offers* itself as a text that is an interpretive key, and unveiling of the secret history … so its not a frame foisted unto the text from outside it\"). The Haran Gawaita is late in its copying (13.4). It presents itself as an unveiling: the hidden history of the Nasoraeans, of the House of the Seven, and of who the prophets of the Lie were. Of the Son of Slaughter it says what he was, \"the most degraded of false prophets\"; which power went with him, \"Mars accompanieth him because he is the Seal of prophets of the Lie\"; and what he did, \"he drew the sword and converted people to himself by the sword\" (13.3). It gives powers their communities: \"for Mercury founded a community in Qum, and Saturn founded a community in Sinai\" (13.3). The text conducts itself as a confession of what happened and what it meant. Its frame is its own. The method of this section (the author, 2026-11-11: \"taking the haran gawaita on its own terms, applying it in an obvious way to revelation that maps directly, and seeing what it yields up\"): the Gawaita is read on its own terms, applied to the Apocalypse where it maps, and read for what it yields (13.6). Its date stands beside every use.\n\n13.1.")
after_para("13.5. ", "13.6. The Gawaita as key to the seven stations. The Apocalypse gives its key at 1:20: \"οἱ ἑπτὰ ἀστέρες ἄγγελοι τῶν ἑπτὰ ἐκκλησιῶν εἰσίν, καὶ αἱ λυχνίαι αἱ ἑπτὰ ἑπτὰ ἐκκλησίαι εἰσίν\", seven powers each over a community. The Gawaita states the same structure as history. A planet founds a community: Mercury at Qum, Saturn at Sinai, and the same passage calls Nazareth \"the city of Qum\" (13.3). A people is \"of the House of the Seven\"; a people leaves \"the Sign of the Seven\"; Ruha's seven are answered by seven guards seated \"upon the seven horns of the worlds\" (13.4). Applied station by station, it yields. (i) Ephesus, Mercury: the Gawaita's Mercury is the lying messiah, \"He is Mercury, who attracteth attention\", with Nbu \"der Lügen-Messias\" in the Ginza (12.1); the Ephesian station is the one that tests those who call themselves sent and are not, \"καὶ εὗρες αὐτοὺς ψευδεῖς\" (2:2). The textual hook of 11.4 is the Gawaita's identification. (ii) Pergamum, Mars: the Gawaita's Mars goes with the prophet who converts by the sword; the Pergamene speaker holds \"τὴν ῥομφαίαν τὴν δίστομον τὴν ὀξεῖαν\" and makes war with it (2:12, 16), at the throne of Satan, where a witness was killed (2:13). The sword that converts is answered by the sword of the mouth. (iii) The seven as a collection: the Gawaita's people of the House of the Seven, and the Nasoraeans who leave the Sign of the Seven, are the condition the letters address, communities under seven powers; the Apocalypse keeps the communities and corrects them (11.3). (iv) The correction itself: the Gawaita's remedy for a seven is a seven, stationed (13.4); the Apocalypse's remedy for each station is a word sent to it (11.1a). Two of the planet–community lines are verified against Drower (13.3). Whether the Gawaita gives each of the seven a community is read in Drower's full text, which is owed against a seated copy (the copy the author supplied on 2026-11-10 is not seated). The Gawaita is the outside source the constraint test asks for (11.5a): profiles of the seven given by a text the archive did not write, a text that offers them as the key.")

# ---- 16. the filing, Wolfson, the dependence argued
sub("Hebrews says in sequence what Part I assembles from Philo across treatises. The paper's work is Philo and the Apocalypse; the Hebrews passage is recorded as the nearest New Testament statement of the cluster.",
    "Hebrews says in sequence what Part I assembles from Philo across treatises, and joins the two Ἰησοῦς in the same verses (7a.8). The dependence of Hebrews on Philo is argued in one literature (Spicq 1952; Thurston 1986; Grindheim 2023) and contested in another (Williamson 1970); either way, the question is heard. The same question for the Apocalypse, on a denser and transformed cluster, was filed (16.6). It is argued in 16.7.")
after_para("16.5a. ", "16.5b. Wolfson, \"The Pre-existent Angel of the Magharians and al-Nahawandi\" (Jewish Quarterly Review, pp. 89–106 as printed in the author's copy; volume and year, n.s. 51, 1960, from the standard citation, to be checked; OCR spacing normalized). The Magharians, \"cave-dwellers\", a Jewish sect Kirkisani places before the rise of Christianity, \"because their books were found in a cave\", with a chief book ascribed to \"the Alexandrian\", held that the world was made by a pre-existent angel, the God of Scripture's anthropomorphic descriptions, \"ultimately sent down by God in the form of man.\" When the source of the view was first asked, \"the answer given was that it was the Logos of Philo.\" Wolfson excludes it on chronological grounds and adds: \"Philo never called the Logos an angel, though he occasionally interprets the term angels in Scripture as Logoi.\" The seated text has the Logos as angel and as archangel: \"τὸν ἀγγέλων πρεσβύτατον, ὡς ἂν ἀρχάγγελον\" (Conf. 146); \"τῷ δὲ ἀρχαγγέλῳ καὶ πρεσβυτάτῳ λόγῳ\" (Her. 205); \"τὸν δὲ ἄγγελον, ὅς ἐστι λόγος\" (Leg. 3.177). The join was made at the first asking and filed by a statement the seated text does not bear out. The filing bears on the Apocalypse, which opens \"διὰ τοῦ ἀγγέλου αὐτοῦ\" (1:1) and speaks to seven ἄγγελοι. Wolfson also records the attempt to identify the Magharians with the sect of Qumran (Golb 1960, reported).")
sub("The warrant he used is inspectable. The letters do not yet meet it: 11.4 gives a distributed configuration, seven fits, and Charles's warrant was a sequence with a direction. Two different evidentiary structures are in play, and the notebook keeps them apart. The letters earn Charles's warrant only if an ordering principle is shown; until then the test for them is the constraint test of 11.5a.",
    "The warrant he used is inspectable, and the letters meet it in the form he required. The governing mapping, read against the Chaldean order, runs in three downward pairs climbing from the lowest spheres to the highest and closes on the middle (11.5b): a sequence with a direction. The commentators had the number at 1:12 and did not read the order of 2–3 against it. The constraint test of 11.5a measures how far the assignment that yields the sequence is fixed by the letters themselves.")
after_para("16.6. ", "16.7. The dependence, argued. The conventional judgment first, in full: the shared features descend from common sources, the lampstand of Exodus, the lamps and eyes of Zechariah, Ezekiel's robed figure and Daniel's son of man, and from a common stock of astral symbolism, so that the Seer had them without Philo (16.1–16.2). Against it, on the criteria under which the dependence of Hebrews is heard (16.4a): (a) Specificity. The features are Philo's readings of the sources, beyond the sources: the lamps as the seven planets with the sun in the middle (Her. 221–224), and a face \"ὡς ὁ ἥλιος\" on the one in the midst (1:16); the planets as \"seven stars\" (Cher. 22; Rev 1:16, 20); the ποδήρης as the robe of the Logos-high priest (Vit. Mos. II.117–121; Fug. 108–110; Rev 1:13); the ῥομφαία as Logos and sun (Cher. 26, 28), honed sharp (Her. 130; Rev 1:16; 2:12); ἀρχή as the first name of the Logos (Conf. 146; Rev 3:14); the messengers as eyes sent over the earth (Somn. 1.140; Rev 5:6); the Logos as angel and archangel (16.5b; Rev 1:1); the Logos named from an oracle spoken to Ἰησοῦς the high priest (Conf. 62; 4.4). (b) Clustering. They gather on one figure in one scene (1:12–20) and its first letter (2:1), and most of them stand in one treatise of Philo (Her. 130, 205, 215–225). (c) Transformation. The one lampstand becomes seven (11.1b); the condemning vote becomes the white one (7b.9); the name restricted to the purified becomes the name held by its receiver (7b.7a); the withheld name is written (7a.9, 7b.10). Each transformation operates on the Philonic reading, and presupposes it in hand. (d) Order. The letters run the stations in a sequence with a direction, closing on the middle (11.5b), which is Charles's warrant for design (16.6). Charles himself grants that the Apocalypse's Logos \"presupposes, while opposing, Philonic speculations\" (16.2). On these grounds the paper states the dependence: the opening vision of the Apocalypse and its seven letters are built on the Philonic construction of the lampstand, the high priest and the Logos. What stays open is the route, Philo's text or a tradition that carried his reading. The claim stands on any date for the Apocalypse after Philo wrote; the paper decides no date (0.5).")

# ---- 17. result
sub("(m) Bousset and Charles printed Philo beside the lampstands; Charles credited design where he had a sequence (21:19–20) and inheritance where he had a number (1:12) (§16).",
    "(m) Bousset and Charles printed Philo beside the lampstands; Charles credited design where he had a sequence (21:19–20) and inheritance where he had a number (1:12) (§16). (n) The Septuagint speaks the name Ἀνατολή twice, both times to Ἰησοῦς the high priest, son of Iosedek (Zech 3:8; 6:11–12); Philo names neither him nor Zechariah, and every Ἰησοῦς in Philo is the son of Nun (2.1, 2.6, 4.1a). (o) The two Septuagint wearers of the ποδήρης in a vision are Ezekiel's man in the midst of six and Zechariah's Ἰησοῦς (Ezek 9:2; Zech 3:4); the Apocalypse's one in the midst wears it and is named Ἰησοῦς (7a.9). (p) Justin joins the two Ἰησοῦς by name and calls the vision of Zechariah's high priest an ἀποκάλυψις (Dial. 115.4; 116.1). (q) Philo's divine ψῆφος is the vote of condemnation; the white pebble is the vote of acquittal (7b.1, 7b.9). (r) The governing mapping read against the Chaldean order gives three downward pairs, climbing, and the Sun last (11.5b). (s) Wolfson's statement that Philo never called the Logos an angel is not borne out by Conf. 146, Her. 205 and Leg. 3.177 (16.5b).")
replace_para("17.2. ", "17.2. Not established: that Philo had the addressee in mind when he cut the citation (the cut is established, 17.1n); that Philo knew of any Jesus movement; that Justin read Philo; that Zechariah's Ἀνατολή is the crowned Ἰησοῦς; the route of the dependence argued in 16.7, Philo's text or a tradition carrying it; the planetary assignments of Smyrna, Sardis and Philadelphia beyond the mapping (11.4); the constraint test of 11.5a, owed, now with the measure of 11.5b; whether the Haran Gawaita gives a community to each of the seven (13.6); that the epistles answer the commission, which is the author's reading, with the downstream sequence of 15.3 the material on which it is tested against the received chronology. Each is stated where it is used.")
after_para("17.2. ", "17.2a. Argued, on the evidence assembled: the dependence of 16.7; the withheld name as a socket the receiver fills, and the Apocalypse's written name as that socket given (4.4a, 7b.10); the Gawaita as the outside key to the seven stations (13.6).")

